Earthline treats correction as part of the method, not as an embarrassment to hide. Errors are recorded; corrections are versioned; earlier reports retain their original method identity; every change declares whether science, data, rendering or wording changed; and field outcomes may become documented evidence records as the validation network grows.</p><div class=\"el49-callout\"><b>Rule:</b> prior records are not silently rewritten. A correction belongs to a dated/versioned method or report release so a reviewer can tell exactly what changed.</div><h3>Current corrected or expressly rejected claims</h3><table class=\"el49-table el12-corrections-table\"><thead><tr><th>Claim / practice</th><th>Earthline correction</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>“1% more organic matter holds 20,000 gallons per acre.”</b></td><td>Not used. The primary synthesis cited in this report puts the effect near 3,400 gallons per acre and concludes the water-storage effect is limited. Earthline bases the soil argument on structure, roots and macropores instead.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Bioswales remove nutrients reliably.</b></td><td>Not claimed. The primary stormwater monitoring database did not find nutrient outflow concentrations significantly different from inflow across green-infrastructure types.</td></tr><tr><td><b>A bioswale is flood protection for a design storm.</b></td><td>Not claimed. Attenuation benefit falls sharply as events become larger. Earthline presents bioswales as distributed retention and timing infrastructure, not a substitute for engineered flood protection.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Salted road runoff should be infiltrated for recharge.</b></td><td>Rejected. Chloride and sodium are poorly attenuated in soil; a swale receiving salted road runoff can become an injection pathway to groundwater. This is why roads and contaminated contributing areas are exclusion concerns.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Urban bioretention performance is a prediction for a contour swale.</b></td><td>Rejected. Engineered bioretention figures are shown only as mechanism/scale context and an upper bound, not as a property-level prediction.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Comparable projects validate Earthline's placement engine.</b></td><td>Rejected. Comparable projects establish mechanism precedent. Validation requires independent/field comparison of Earthline's actual locations, exclusions and outcomes.</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"el49-source\">Corrections governance: Earthline Screening Method v1.0 §15.5 and §16. Claim entries: How Bioswales Work v2.0 §§6.4, 6.7, 6.8 and §9.</p></section>";
